  "documentation": {
    "range": "The species is widely distributed in the Amazon River basin (Brazil, Colombia, and Peru),&#160;Rio Oyapock in French Guiana, Essequibo River in Guyana, and is introduced in Suriname (Froese and Pauly 2019, Fricke <em>et al</em>. 2020).",
    "population": "<p>There are no data on population size and trends for this species, but it appears to be common and abundant.</p>",
    "habitats": "<span>The species inhabit swamps or flooded grounds where the aquatic and riverine  vegetation are dense and the water is either clear or silty.  Its colour&#160;is deeper in clear water.<span> Males court females during the breeding season. Both male and  female guard the eggs which are attached to the surface of aquatic  vegetation in a nest area.  During the entire brooding cycle, bonding of the original pair of  parents is maintained complete with defence of each partner against  aggression or potential rivals (Froese and Pauly 2019).\t\t\t\t<span></span>",
    "threats": "<p>There is no information about threats to this species.</p>",
    "measures": "<p>There is no information on conservation actions in place or recommended for this species.</p>",
    "use_trade": "The species is used heavily in the aquarium trade.",
    "rationale": "<p>Despite there being little information available on this species, it is common and abundant and has a wide range, which is unlikely to have severe threats. It is therefore assessed as Least Concern.</p>",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "<p>There are no relevant taxonomic limitations.</p>"
  },
